'''Sant Jorge''' is a town in the [[La Taula]] comarca of [[Tramuntana Province]], [[Balboa]]. With a population of 12,204 (2026 estimate), it is the second-largest settlement in the comarca after the comarca capital [[Sant Martí del Nord]] (14,420). Sant Jorge is located in the far north of the Balboa isthmus, in the highland interior of Tramuntana Province, at an elevation of approximately 800 metres above sea level on the Shendan-facing slope of the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range|Yalabirga]] range.

The town is one of the northernmost settlements in Balboa and serves as a local centre for the surrounding highland communities. The [[Sant Jorge Airport]], a turboprop-only domestic facility, connects the town to the national air network via [[Portblanc Airport|Portblanc]] on the [[Aeroístmus]] route from [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport|Sant Cristòfor]].

== Geography ==

Sant Jorge is situated in the northern highlands of Tramuntana Province, on the western (Shendan) slope of the Yalabirga range. The terrain around the town is rugged, with steep valleys and forested ridges characterising the landscape. The town itself occupies a relatively flat basin at approximately 800 metres elevation, one of the few areas of level ground in the northern highlands, which made it a natural site for settlement.

The climate is cooler and drier than the coastal lowlands, with a pronounced wet season from May to November. The surrounding highlands receive approximately 1,000–1,500 mm of rainfall annually, supporting montane forest and limited agriculture in the valley floors. The Shendan-facing slope lies in the rain shadow of the higher Yalabirga peaks to the east, resulting in drier conditions than the Aethiopian side of the range.

The town is approximately 120 km north of [[Portblanc]], the provincial capital, connected by a regional road that winds through the highland valleys. The journey by road takes approximately four hours. The [[Sant Martí del Nord Airport]], serving the comarca capital, is located approximately 60 km to the northeast.

== History ==

The area around Sant Jorge has been inhabited by the [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] for centuries. The Birú retreated into the northern highlands following the Volisanian conquest of the isthmian coast and maintained communities in relative isolation throughout the colonial period. The town's name, derived from the Volisanian name for [[St. George|Sant Jorge]], suggests that the settlement was established or formalised during the colonial era, likely as a Volisanian missionary or administrative outpost in the highland interior.

During the colonial period, the northern highlands of Tramuntana were among the least integrated territories in the Balboan isthmus. The region's remoteness, difficult terrain, and sparse population meant that colonial authority was limited to occasional visits by provincial officials and clergy. The Birú population maintained a degree of autonomy through the colonial and republican periods, though the expansion of cattle ranching and logging into the highland valleys in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries gradually encroached on traditional lands.

The construction of the [[Sant Jorge Airport]] in the post-reunification period provided the first reliable year-round transport link to the town, replacing the seasonal road access that had previously been the only connection to the provincial network. The airport operates as an essential services destination, receiving subsidies under the national air connectivity programme.

== Economy ==

The economy of Sant Jorge is based primarily on subsistence agriculture, small-scale cattle ranching, and limited forestry. The surrounding highland valleys support the cultivation of maize, beans, and root vegetables, primarily for local consumption. Cattle ranching on the Tramuntana savanna to the west of the town provides a small surplus for sale in regional markets. The town has limited commercial activity, with a weekly market serving the surrounding rural communities.

The town has a health clinic, a primary school, and a secondary school serving the comarca's southern communities. The municipal council provides basic services, though infrastructure remains limited compared to the provincial average. The provincial government has identified the La Taula comarca as a priority area for development assistance under the national rural development programme.

== Indigenous community ==

Sant Jorge is located in proximity to several [[Birú people|Birú]] communities in the northern highlands. The Birú population in the area has raised concerns about land use, environmental impact, and the disruption of cultural sites in connection with development projects. In 2026, proposed construction of a data center facility near the town by [[Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses]] drew sustained opposition from indigenous communities and leftist political parties, highlighting the ongoing tension between infrastructure development and indigenous land rights in the Tramuntana highlands.

== Transport ==

{{main|Sant Jorge Airport}}

[[Sant Jorge Airport]] (IATA: SJD, ICAO: SJIB) is a domestic turboprop-only airport located near the town. The airport is served by [[Aeroístmus]] from [[Portblanc Airport|Portblanc]] as a stop on the route from [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport|Sant Cristòfor]], operating under the national essential services programme. The airport handled approximately 55,000 passengers in 2025.

Road connections to Sant Jorge are limited to a regional road linking the town to Portblanc to the south and to [[Sant Martí del Nord]] to the northeast. The road to Portblanc is paved but narrow, with frequent landslides during the wet season. The road to Sant Martí del Nord is partially unpaved and is frequently impassable during the wet season.

